ity and concluded that making the call would be a negative-EV play.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The opponent has effectively gone all-in pre-flop on the very first hand. My hand, 6 of Spades and 5 of Hearts (6s5h), is a weak unsuited hand. Against a random hand, 6s5h has approximately 38% equity. Against a reasonable shoving range (pairs, high cards), the equity is significantly lower. To justify calling an all-in bet, I would need approximately 50% equity given the near 1:1 pot odds (risking my stack to double up). Since 38% < 50%, calling has a negative expected value. It is strategically unsound to risk the entire tournament life on a weak hand with negative equity on the first round.\n\nReason: My hand is statistically dominated by the vast majority of possible opponent hands. Calling an all-in with 6-5 offsuit is a losing play in the long run. Preserving chips for a better hand is the correct strategy.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
